1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
They're the power that delivers the goods,

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
and the speed that pushes physics.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
They connect oceans and move men deep below the Earth's surface.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
They're crashed for safety and built to last,

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
powered by diesel, electricity, or steam.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
They come in all shapes and sizes.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Some even ride on air.

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,700
shapes and sizes. Some even ride on air.

9
00:00:30,701 --> 00:00:35,700
Now, the untold story of locomotives on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,300
Witness the pride of France, the

11
00:00:50,301 --> 00:00:55,300
or TGV.

12
00:00:55,301 --> 00:00:58,500
Translated, it means high-speed train.

13
00:00:58,501 --> 00:01:00,460
But as with all trains, it's the locomotive

14
00:01:00,461 --> 00:01:02,660
that delivers the power.

15
00:01:02,661 --> 00:01:05,900
And as it rips across its 1,100-mile network,

16
00:01:05,901 --> 00:01:09,600
the TGV shows why it holds the title of the fastest

17
00:01:09,601 --> 00:01:12,600
locomotive on rails today.

18
00:01:12,601 --> 00:01:15,140
If you can figure out, we are running now

19
00:01:15,141 --> 00:01:18,160
at 300 kilometers per hour.

20
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:20,660
It seems like a dream.

21
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:22,200
It's very easy.

22
00:01:22,201 --> 00:01:25,000
186 miles per hour is the average speed

23
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:27,760
across much of the TGV network.

24
00:01:27,761 --> 00:01:32,260
The high-tension wires racing by deliver the TGV's power.

25
00:01:32,261 --> 00:01:35,260
From them, 25,000 volts of electricity

26
00:01:35,261 --> 00:01:38,100
is transformed into mechanical energy, which is then

27
00:01:38,101 --> 00:01:39,760
sent to motors on the train's wheels.

28
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:47,560
Even at speeds like this inside the TGV,

29
00:01:47,561 --> 00:01:49,260
The ride is smooth and steady.

30
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:52,260
But if the engineer spots something on the track outside,

31
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,800
there's little he can do to avoid hitting it.

32
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:58,700
If you see something, then you will just knock it down.

33
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:02,340
Running at 300 kilometers, you would need

34
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:04,140
three kilometers to stop.

35
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:09,880
So if you can see something, obviously it's too late.

36
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:13,120
In 1990, the TGV set the world speed record

37
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:17,220
when it raced to an astounding 320 miles per hour.

38
00:02:17,221 --> 00:02:21,620
But by 2007, train officials wanted more speed.

39
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:24,320
So after some modifications, a TGV locomotive

40
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:26,620
was unleashed for another record attempt.

41
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:39,680
To beat the existing 320 mile per hour record,

42
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:45,620
the TGV would have to exceed 516 kilometers per hour.

43
00:02:45,621 --> 00:02:46,620
460.

44
00:02:54,020 --> 00:02:56,020
480 plus 5.

45
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:01,820
1000 mètres.

46
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:03,020
105.

47
00:03:03,021 --> 00:03:04,020
515.

48
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:44,940
574 kilometers per hour equals 357 miles per hour,

49
00:03:44,941 --> 00:03:46,980
and a new world record.

50
00:03:46,981 --> 00:03:49,080
The remarkable run proved once again

51
00:03:49,081 --> 00:03:54,040
that when it comes to speed, the TGV is king of the rails.

52
00:03:54,041 --> 00:03:58,680
The TGV might be the fastest locomotive on rails today,

53
00:03:58,681 --> 00:04:01,080
but this is what high-speed locomotives used to look like.

54
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:06,940
During the early 20th century, locomotives rarely

55
00:04:06,941 --> 00:04:09,420
hit 100 miles per hour.

56
00:04:09,421 --> 00:04:12,320
But when it comes to looks, many locomotive fans

57
00:04:12,321 --> 00:04:16,820
that none have been more smokin' than those powered by steam.

58
00:04:16,821 --> 00:04:22,820
Like this beauty built in 1924, the Baldwin Company's number 90,

59
00:04:22,821 --> 00:04:24,820
still running on the Strasburg Railroad

60
00:04:24,821 --> 00:04:27,820
in Strasburg, Pennsylvania.

61
00:04:27,821 --> 00:04:30,820
Today, it's a tourist train, so it's still possible

62
00:04:30,821 --> 00:04:32,820
to climb on board for a ride back

63
00:04:32,821 --> 00:04:36,820
to when locomotives like this helped pull America full steam

64
00:04:36,821 --> 00:04:40,320
ahead into the 20th century.

65
00:04:40,321 --> 00:04:42,320
Now we're ready to depart.

66
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:44,220
Now I'm going to release the brakes

67
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:49,320
and apply steam to the shoulders through the throttle.

68
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:53,560
Now it's my job not to spin the wheel,

69
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:56,160
so I want to just gradually apply power.

70
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:05,880
Applying power requires teamwork

71
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,680
between the engineer and the man to his left

72
00:05:08,681 --> 00:05:11,480
shoveling coal from the tender into the boiler,

73
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:12,920
the fireman.

74
00:05:13,020 --> 00:05:17,720
Now the fireman has to get his fire nice and hot.

75
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:21,320
By experience, he knows when to shovel.

76
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:23,120
It's a coordinated effort.

77
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:26,040
When I need this steam, he has to already have planned

78
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:28,340
on producing.

79
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:32,740
In the past, you'd burn a bow shot.

80
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:35,340
A ton and a half of coal per hour,

81
00:05:35,341 --> 00:05:38,840
and a fireman would be on duty for 12 hours.

82
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:42,820
It could burn upwards of 12 to 15 tons of coal a day

83
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:46,160
to shovel in by hand from the tender.

84
00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:47,880
Igniting coal in the boiler

85
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:51,360
is the first step in producing power.

86
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:54,620
Since our camera would be roasted by the 2,500-degree

87
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:58,300
temperature in there, the best way to see how a steam engine

88
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:02,240
works is by squeezing into a typical locomotive boiler.

89
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:03,940
This one is currently under repair

90
00:06:03,941 --> 00:06:05,980
under repair inside the Strasburg Railroad

91
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:07,980
mechanical shop.

92
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,580
Fireman throws the coal through the hole in the rear,

93
00:06:10,680 --> 00:06:11,880
lands in this box.

94
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:14,080
This box is called the firebox.

95
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:17,380
Surrounding the firebox is water.

96
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:20,280
The water is all the way around here and is normally

97
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:22,000
carried up to here.

98
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:24,500
It always has to be above the top of the firebox.

99
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:26,900
If it's not above the top of the firebox, the top of the

100
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,200
firebox will melt.

101
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:33,300
So the heat from the fire can heat the firebox steel,

102
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:37,000
boil the water, create the steam that goes up into the steam

103
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:40,800
space, goes up into the steam dome.

104
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:43,240
Within the steam dome is a throttle.

105
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:45,640
When the engineer engages the throttle lever,

106
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:48,840
steam is released into a pipe, where it travels

107
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:51,360
to the locomotive cylinders.

108
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:56,260
Inside the cylinders, the steam pushes pistons back and forth.

109
00:06:56,360 --> 00:06:59,200
The pistons drive piston rods that, in turn,

110
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:02,260
drive the locomotive's wheels.

111
00:07:02,261 --> 00:07:05,700
Regulating the steam pressure inside the boiler is critical.

112
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,560
Too little, and the train loses momentum.

113
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:12,300
Too much, and the boiler could explode.

114
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:15,100
The steam boiler's design was inventive.

115
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:17,240
But because coal is fed from the rear,

116
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:20,120
it creates a problem for the engineer.

117
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:23,680
I can't see very well past this huge boiler.

118
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:26,820
I have to lean out so I can see the biggest perspective

119
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:28,160
ahead of the engine.

120
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:30,560
I am constantly looking for anything

121
00:07:30,561 --> 00:07:34,260
that might obstruct us, including trees, including

122
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:38,560
cattle, or anything else that might be problematic.

123
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:40,100
That's why locomotive designers

124
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:43,600
invented the cow catcher.

125
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:46,400
OK, it doesn't exactly catch cows.

126
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:48,920
It kind of muscles them aside.

127
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:51,920
But the cow catcher can't shove aside the effects of the inevitable

128
00:07:52,020 --> 00:07:56,220
wear and tear that comes with a 100-ton locomotive thrashing

129
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:57,920
around on two-inch wide rails.

130
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:02,560
especially take a beating.

131
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:06,360
Like a car, a locomotive tire is attached to a wheel.

132
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:08,900
Except these tires are made of steel,

133
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,200
and changing one takes some heat.

134
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:17,140
The tire of a steam locomotive is shrunk onto the wheel.

135
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:20,540
So in order to remove it, we put the fire ring on,

136
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:24,620
we heat the tire up probably to 400 or 500 degrees.

137
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:27,460
As it gets hotter, it will expand.

138
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:29,820
And eventually, there'll be a gap between the tire

139
00:08:29,821 --> 00:08:34,620
and the wheel, and that tire will be able to swing and slip

140
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:38,560
right off the wheel center.

141
00:08:38,660 --> 00:08:40,860
Add a fresh steel tire to the wheel,

142
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:42,960
and it'll be ready to roll.

143
00:08:43,060 --> 00:08:46,340
Easy enough inside the mechanical shop.

144
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:49,540
But in the old days when things broke down out on the planes,

145
00:08:49,640 --> 00:08:52,480
the engineer did double duty as the mechanic.

146
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:56,180
The technology was constantly breaking down.

147
00:08:56,280 --> 00:08:59,580
The engineer had to be aware of any problem arising

148
00:08:59,581 --> 00:09:02,920
by both sound and vision.

149
00:09:03,020 --> 00:09:05,520
And when that vision spotted something on the track,

150
00:09:05,620 --> 00:09:06,980
it was time to sound off.

151
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:12,120
Every time the toot of a steam locomotive's whistle

152
00:09:12,220 --> 00:09:17,140
fills the air, it speaks in a unique, yet universal language.

153
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:20,040
Now, every engineer does his whistling

154
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:21,940
a little bit differently.

155
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:26,540
You'll now hear a crossing signal two longs, a short,

156
00:09:26,541 --> 00:09:29,040
and along as we approach this crossing.

157
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,700
By the 1930s, the sounds of the steam locomotive

158
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:51,700
began to fade as designers looked to new technologies.

159
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:56,100
In 1934, ElectroMotive launched a line of diesel-electric

160
00:09:56,101 --> 00:09:58,940
electric streamliner locomotives that were easier

161
00:09:58,941 --> 00:10:03,540
to maintain and cheaper to fuel than the coal-fed steamers.

162
00:10:03,541 --> 00:10:05,300
Today, diesel electric locomotives

163
00:10:05,301 --> 00:10:07,240
have a different look.

164
00:10:07,241 --> 00:10:11,000
But they are still the industry standard.

165
00:10:11,001 --> 00:10:14,300
Companies like Electromotive Diesel in London, Ontario,

166
00:10:14,301 --> 00:10:15,980
build hundreds of them every year

167
00:10:15,981 --> 00:10:18,400
for the international market.

168
00:10:18,401 --> 00:10:20,900
And every one starts from the bottom up

169
00:10:20,901 --> 00:10:23,400
with a single steel bed plate.

170
00:10:23,401 --> 00:10:26,660
What we see here is a domestic bed plate.

171
00:10:26,661 --> 00:10:29,740
It's approximately 1 and 3 quarter inches thick,

172
00:10:29,741 --> 00:10:33,060
70 feet long, and 6 feet wide.

173
00:10:33,061 --> 00:10:36,580
And it weighs approximately 30,000 pounds.

174
00:10:36,581 --> 00:10:39,960
The bed plate begins its journey upside down,

175
00:10:39,961 --> 00:10:42,440
so welders and grinders can add sills and cross

176
00:10:42,441 --> 00:10:45,460
members to the underside.

177
00:10:45,461 --> 00:10:49,300
The locomotive's underframe is taking shape.

178
00:10:49,301 --> 00:10:53,700
Then it's up, up, and away to do the locomotion.

179
00:10:53,701 --> 00:10:56,240
The underframe itself is almost 80,000 pounds

180
00:10:56,241 --> 00:10:57,800
at this point.

181
00:10:57,801 --> 00:11:00,080
The upside down underframe eventually

182
00:11:00,081 --> 00:11:03,260
lands in the assembly area.

183
00:11:03,261 --> 00:11:07,340
Here, some of the major components are lowered into place.

184
00:11:07,341 --> 00:11:09,440
So what we see here is the compressed air

185
00:11:09,441 --> 00:11:12,160
tanks that supply the compressed air for the locomotive as well

186
00:11:12,161 --> 00:11:13,360
as the rail cars.

187
00:11:13,361 --> 00:11:16,840
And above us, what we see is the loading

188
00:11:16,841 --> 00:11:18,860
of a 5,000 gallon fuel tank, which

189
00:11:18,861 --> 00:11:22,620
our domestic fuel tank. Tighten a few bolts, and the

190
00:11:22,621 --> 00:11:26,200
underside of the locomotive is just about locked down.

191
00:11:26,201 --> 00:11:33,400
Meanwhile, workers are assembling other parts throughout the plant, like this upside-down

192
00:11:33,401 --> 00:11:39,209
wheel assembly, a combination of wheels, gear, axle, and electric motor.

193
00:11:39,210 --> 00:11:40,580
This is where the

194
00:11:40,581 --> 00:11:43,860
electric part of the diesel-electric locomotive comes in.

195
00:11:43,861 --> 00:11:48,300
We got a 1,000-pound wheel at a 43-inch diameter.

196
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:53,440
We got an 83-tooth gear that weighs approximately 500 pounds

197
00:11:53,441 --> 00:11:56,720
with a 9-1⁄4-inch diameter axle

198
00:11:56,721 --> 00:11:58,860
sitting on an electric traction motor.

199
00:11:59,820 --> 00:12:01,560
Put three wheel assemblies together,

200
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:02,860
and you've got a bogey.

201
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:05,880
And this bogey is about to take its first spin,

202
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:09,300
up and over until it's right side up.

203
00:12:10,700 --> 00:12:12,480
Then it's off to final assembly,

204
00:12:12,481 --> 00:12:15,380
where the underframe is taking a twirl of its own.

205
00:12:16,440 --> 00:12:18,880
Set the underframe on top of a set of bogies,

206
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:20,780
lower the cab into place,

207
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:23,100
and all this loco needs is some motive.

208
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:25,700
Yep, this is the diesel part.

209
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:27,920
What we see here is a diesel engine

210
00:12:27,921 --> 00:12:29,900
that we use on one of our locomotives.

211
00:12:30,740 --> 00:12:32,840
Compared to a normal car engine,

212
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:35,300
a car engine runs around four to six cylinders,

213
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:36,660
roughly about this size,

214
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:38,940
compared to the cylinders that we use here,

215
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:40,320
and we use 16 of them.

216
00:12:40,980 --> 00:12:42,260
When we talk about this engine,

217
00:12:42,261 --> 00:12:44,820
and it weighs approximately 40,000 pounds

218
00:12:44,821 --> 00:12:46,200
compared to a local car engine,

219
00:12:46,201 --> 00:12:48,420
which is much less than that, obviously.

220
00:12:48,421 --> 00:12:51,900
Also, a local car engine would take around 200 horsepower,

221
00:12:51,901 --> 00:12:53,980
whereas this guy right here, our workhorse,

222
00:12:53,981 --> 00:12:56,840
runs around 4,300 horsepower.

223
00:12:56,841 --> 00:12:59,540
From here, we use the overhead cranes

224
00:12:59,541 --> 00:13:01,420
to remove the engine off the stand

225
00:13:01,421 --> 00:13:05,160
and deck it onto one of our units.

226
00:13:05,161 --> 00:13:07,020
On board, mechanics hook up the engine

227
00:13:07,021 --> 00:13:10,620
to an eight-ton alternator,

228
00:13:10,621 --> 00:13:14,080
and the diesel electric locomotive is ready to rumble.

229
00:13:14,081 --> 00:13:18,080
And when it does, here's how it will work.

230
00:13:18,081 --> 00:13:21,380
The diesel engine sends mechanical power to the alternator.

231
00:13:21,381 --> 00:13:24,780
The alternator transforms that power into electricity.

232
00:13:24,781 --> 00:13:27,420
The electricity is then sent to the electric traction motors

233
00:13:27,421 --> 00:13:30,420
on each wheel assembly.

234
00:13:30,421 --> 00:13:35,240
The motors turn the wheels, which apply power to the track.

235
00:13:35,241 --> 00:13:37,400
In the locomotive business, power on the track

236
00:13:37,401 --> 00:13:40,420
is measured in tractive effort.

237
00:13:40,421 --> 00:13:42,840
The more traction that can be applied between wheel and rail,

238
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:45,260
the more pulling power on the locomotive.

239
00:13:46,680 --> 00:13:48,880
Central detractive effort is managing friction.

240
00:13:49,820 --> 00:13:52,260
And the secret lies in the simplest of substances.

241
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:56,440
What we want here is we want to have good friction

242
00:13:56,441 --> 00:14:00,680
on the surface of the wheel where it contacts the actual rail.

243
00:14:00,681 --> 00:14:03,120
So how we increase the frictional force

244
00:14:03,121 --> 00:14:05,920
between these two surfaces is we use this nozzle

245
00:14:05,921 --> 00:14:09,820
and we actually pour sand into this area.

246
00:14:10,420 --> 00:14:13,220
What that does is it increases the frictional force,

247
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:14,820
thus allowing more tractive effort

248
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:17,160
to be placed on the rail.

249
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:18,660
While the sand increases friction

250
00:14:18,760 --> 00:14:22,760
to the bottom of the wheel, just inches away,

251
00:14:22,860 --> 00:14:26,100
the wheel flange gets a rub with a clever little device

252
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:27,940
that decreases friction.

253
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:30,140
Here's the frictional force that we don't want.

254
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:32,700
That's between this surface of the wheel

255
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:34,920
and the inner surface of the rail.

256
00:14:35,020 --> 00:14:36,980
So what we've done to counteract that

257
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:38,980
is we have this flange lube dispenser

258
00:14:38,981 --> 00:14:42,280
that has a graphite-based lubricant that makes contact

259
00:14:42,281 --> 00:14:45,280
with this surface. What it does is it applies it

260
00:14:45,281 --> 00:14:48,820
as the wheel turns and thus reduces the frictional force

261
00:14:48,821 --> 00:14:53,020
between this surface of the wheel and the rail itself.

262
00:14:53,021 --> 00:14:56,340
After all the steel is secured, the bogies are bolted,

263
00:14:56,341 --> 00:15:00,140
and the engine and motors are mounted.

264
00:15:00,141 --> 00:15:02,340
They make up one of the largest freight locomotives

265
00:15:02,341 --> 00:15:06,480
in the world. Here we are next to a completed

266
00:15:06,481 --> 00:15:07,480
locomotive.

267
00:15:07,481 --> 00:15:14,480
The locomotive is 75 feet long, 16 feet high, and 10 feet wide.

268
00:15:14,481 --> 00:15:19,420
The locomotive itself weighs 425,000 pounds.

269
00:15:19,421 --> 00:15:23,040
It's really the workhorse out there in the industry.

270
00:15:23,041 --> 00:15:27,040
But before each of its workhorses is turned loose,

271
00:15:27,041 --> 00:15:29,540
it gets ridden hard out on the test track.

272
00:15:29,541 --> 00:15:31,740
So there's full throttle eight.

273
00:15:31,741 --> 00:15:35,040
You see the tractive effort build up.

274
00:15:35,041 --> 00:15:38,020
And I should get around 4,500 horsepower.

275
00:15:38,860 --> 00:15:39,560
This is good.

276
00:15:40,220 --> 00:15:41,160
This is a good engine.

277
00:15:41,700 --> 00:15:42,620
It'll pull like crazy.

278
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:44,100
Crazy?

279
00:15:45,260 --> 00:15:46,660
How's this for crazy?

280
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:51,340
Nothing like a header to find a locomotive's weak spot.

281
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:58,380
A modern diesel electric locomotive can move one ton of freight

282
00:15:58,381 --> 00:16:01,480
an average of 202 miles on a gallon of diesel fuel.

283
00:16:01,481 --> 00:16:04,280
a modern 18-wheeler truck can only move a ton of freight

284
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:08,580
59 miles on the same gallon.

285
00:16:08,680 --> 00:16:12,320
This is TTCI's Transportation Technology Center

286
00:16:12,420 --> 00:16:15,920
outside Pueblo, Colorado, the place where the Federal Railroad

287
00:16:16,020 --> 00:16:19,040
Administration conducts research, and locomotive

288
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:21,740
manufacturers send their technicians

289
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:27,780
to test their latest designs, to watch train wrecks,

290
00:16:27,781 --> 00:16:31,620
and to study the effects for safety and future development.

291
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:34,620
One of the things that we do here at TTCI

292
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:36,580
is to conduct crash tests.

293
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:39,000
That's one of the abilities that we have

294
00:16:39,100 --> 00:16:40,660
at an isolated site like that.

295
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:44,160
And we've done that right on the tracks right behind me.

296
00:16:44,260 --> 00:16:47,960
In the real world, locomotive crashes like this are rare.

297
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:50,240
And whether the other vehicle is a car or truck

298
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:54,180
that tried to run a crossing, or a train that was mistakenly

299
00:16:54,280 --> 00:16:57,100
switched to the wrong track, the outcome is always

300
00:16:57,101 --> 00:16:58,100
the same.

301
00:16:58,200 --> 00:17:00,600
The locomotive wins.

302
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:03,100
As this crash test shows, the damage caused

303
00:17:03,200 --> 00:17:05,900
by the locomotive climbing up and over the train car

304
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,340
is extensive and possibly life-threatening to the engineers

305
00:17:09,440 --> 00:17:11,640
sitting in the cab.

306
00:17:11,740 --> 00:17:16,660
By studying crash test video, manufacturers

307
00:17:16,760 --> 00:17:19,260
like Electromotive Diesel, or EMD,

308
00:17:19,360 --> 00:17:23,960
are able to develop ways to reduce the risks.

309
00:17:24,060 --> 00:17:26,300
Even during the mother of all crashes,

310
00:17:26,301 --> 00:17:29,340
a head-on between two locomotives.

311
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:33,340
For head-on collisions, we've developed an anticlimber.

312
00:17:33,440 --> 00:17:36,440
This whole structure is the anticlimber.

313
00:17:36,540 --> 00:17:41,140
It is designed to resist a strong upward force here and here

314
00:17:41,240 --> 00:17:44,860
and keep the coupler from coming up over the main structure here

315
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:48,260
and the other car going into the cab.

316
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:50,460
When two locomotives without the anticlimber

317
00:17:50,560 --> 00:17:53,800
collide head-on, the locomotive with more momentum

318
00:17:53,801 --> 00:17:56,000
can slide straight up and into the other's cab.

319
00:17:58,900 --> 00:18:01,600
But if one locomotive has an anticlimber,

320
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:03,140
the coupler from the other locomotive

321
00:18:03,240 --> 00:18:05,340
catches underneath the anticlimber,

322
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:08,840
stopping upward motion and diverting the energy laterally.

323
00:18:12,920 --> 00:18:16,460
The EMD anticlimber, first used in 1989,

324
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:18,900
is now the industry standard.

325
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,700
The crashworthiness of the locomotives used

326
00:18:22,701 --> 00:18:23,700
in North America.

327
00:18:23,701 --> 00:18:26,700
It's the best it's ever been, largely as a result

328
00:18:26,701 --> 00:18:29,100
of the kinds of tests that we do here,

329
00:18:29,101 --> 00:18:31,640
the activities of people like EMD,

330
00:18:31,641 --> 00:18:36,240
in applying that information into the design of their cab.

331
00:18:36,241 --> 00:18:38,940
Out on the TTCI test track,

332
00:18:38,941 --> 00:18:42,920
EMD engineers are evaluating their newest design element,

333
00:18:42,921 --> 00:18:46,860
what they call the isolated cab.

334
00:18:46,861 --> 00:18:50,000
Their goal is to have the quietest cab in the business.

335
00:18:50,001 --> 00:18:52,700
We are trying to control the working environment

336
00:18:52,701 --> 00:18:56,040
The whole purpose is to keep the noise down inside the cab

337
00:18:56,140 --> 00:19:01,040
from the 4,500 horsepower that's just 20 feet behind us.

338
00:19:01,140 --> 00:19:03,740
EMD has tried to reduce the noise and vibration

339
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:06,540
inside the cab by separating it from the engine compartment.

340
00:19:09,380 --> 00:19:11,820
To record the decibel level, technicians

341
00:19:11,920 --> 00:19:14,560
have hung microphones at ear level throughout the cab.

342
00:19:14,660 --> 00:19:17,760
The target is 80 decibels or less.

343
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:19,320
Then it's time to open up the throttle

344
00:19:19,420 --> 00:19:22,700
to see if the isolated mounts work.

345
00:19:22,701 --> 00:19:25,200
Even at full throttle, the sound level

346
00:19:25,201 --> 00:19:28,840
barely exceeds 75 decibels, about the same

347
00:19:28,841 --> 00:19:31,700
as a normal conversation between two people,

348
00:19:31,701 --> 00:19:35,640
and a victory for the EMD designers.

349
00:19:35,641 --> 00:19:37,240
And while they're here, the crew

350
00:19:37,241 --> 00:19:39,240
is also collecting ride quality data

351
00:19:39,241 --> 00:19:42,160
by testing the cab for vibration.

352
00:19:42,161 --> 00:19:44,160
The whole cab has a number of sensors

353
00:19:44,161 --> 00:19:47,160
all over the cab, one of which is right here,

354
00:19:47,161 --> 00:19:51,700
where we measure the cab's seat-based vibration.

355
00:19:51,701 --> 00:19:55,000
in all three directions, up and down, four and a half,

356
00:19:55,100 --> 00:19:56,740
side to side.

357
00:19:56,840 --> 00:19:59,000
Technicians record the data in a test car

358
00:19:59,100 --> 00:20:01,240
behind the locomotive.

359
00:20:01,340 --> 00:20:04,540
Later, EMD will use the data to develop even more ways

360
00:20:04,640 --> 00:20:08,080
to improve its locomotives and keep its operating engineers

361
00:20:08,180 --> 00:20:10,680
safe.

362
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:12,560
In another area of the test facility,

363
00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:14,960
TTCI offers locomotive makers a way

364
00:20:15,060 --> 00:20:19,620
to look through steel with its automated cracked wheel

365
00:20:19,621 --> 00:20:22,860
a cracked wheel detection system.

366
00:20:22,861 --> 00:20:25,260
The system uses sound waves to take a snapshot

367
00:20:25,261 --> 00:20:28,060
of a locomotive's wheels as they pass through an ultrasonic

368
00:20:28,061 --> 00:20:29,660
sensor.

369
00:20:29,661 --> 00:20:32,700
Right now, this first wheel is going to be picked up

370
00:20:32,701 --> 00:20:34,360
by this inspection site.

371
00:20:34,361 --> 00:20:37,080
It's going to follow it all the way through.

372
00:20:37,081 --> 00:20:39,780
Once it follows it through, it's going to reset itself,

373
00:20:39,781 --> 00:20:42,240
set up for the next wheel that comes through.

374
00:20:42,241 --> 00:20:43,240
It'll pick it up.

375
00:20:43,241 --> 00:20:46,720
It'll send ultrasonic energy up through the wheel

376
00:20:46,721 --> 00:20:48,680
and inspect the wheel for cracks.

377
00:20:48,681 --> 00:20:51,380
And then that information will be transmitted

378
00:20:51,381 --> 00:20:53,640
into our computer bungalow.

379
00:20:53,641 --> 00:20:55,140
Inside the bungalow, the results

380
00:20:55,141 --> 00:20:58,100
appear on a computer screen in real time.

381
00:20:58,101 --> 00:21:00,440
Each rectangular box represents a wheel.

382
00:21:00,441 --> 00:21:02,540
The lines reveal some type of flaw.

383
00:21:02,541 --> 00:21:04,940
Right here, you're showing some thermal cracks

384
00:21:04,941 --> 00:21:06,380
in the locomotive wheel.

385
00:21:06,381 --> 00:21:08,440
Here, you're showing a horizontal crack.

386
00:21:08,441 --> 00:21:12,080
So then it triggers the maintenance crew

387
00:21:12,081 --> 00:21:15,420
on what they need to do in order to correct that.

388
00:21:15,421 --> 00:21:18,680
Whereas TTCI exists to help find a locomotive's flaws,

389
00:21:18,681 --> 00:21:22,980
A network of service stations across the United States

390
00:21:23,080 --> 00:21:25,080
stands ready to fix them.

391
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:28,120
One of the best is the CSX maintenance facility

392
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:30,620
in Waycross, Georgia.

393
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:33,720
CSX Transportation runs the largest railroad operation

394
00:21:33,820 --> 00:21:36,340
in the eastern United States.

395
00:21:36,440 --> 00:21:39,140
Every day, thousands of CSX locomotives

396
00:21:39,240 --> 00:21:41,940
traverse the country, hauling freight.

397
00:21:42,040 --> 00:21:45,180
Keeping them operational is crucial to the bottom line.

398
00:21:45,280 --> 00:21:47,640
Time is money, and these locomotives

399
00:21:47,641 --> 00:21:51,440
And these locomotives cost about $2 million apiece.

400
00:21:51,441 --> 00:21:55,280
And if they're setting, we're not utilizing that asset.

401
00:21:55,281 --> 00:21:58,280
We can service as many as 12 at one time.

402
00:21:58,281 --> 00:22:01,720
This is simply a service station for locomotives.

403
00:22:01,721 --> 00:22:04,960
And this service station is built for speed.

404
00:22:04,961 --> 00:22:08,820
It's kind of like a NASCAR pit stop for locomotives.

405
00:22:08,821 --> 00:22:11,540
The moment a locomotive pulls in, a team of workers

406
00:22:11,541 --> 00:22:13,860
moves into place.

407
00:22:13,861 --> 00:22:17,860
The aim is to get it in and out in under an hour.

408
00:22:17,960 --> 00:22:20,600
This is where it starts here at the locomotive services center.

409
00:22:20,700 --> 00:22:23,600
We'll service anywhere from 75 to 100 locomotives

410
00:22:23,700 --> 00:22:25,100
at this facility per day.

411
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:26,600
Here's where the sand will be going in.

412
00:22:26,700 --> 00:22:29,500
It'll be coming in through a funnel that he'll put in.

413
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:31,320
Here we have a gentleman that's going to be changing

414
00:22:31,420 --> 00:22:34,720
a flange lube stick to help with the flange wear.

415
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:36,520
We're going in the curves on the track.

416
00:22:36,620 --> 00:22:39,420
Here the gentleman is checking the oil and the water.

417
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:42,320
We'll make sure everything is in good running repair

418
00:22:42,321 --> 00:22:43,820
with the diesel engine.

419
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:46,720
Here we are adding fuel to the locomotive.

420
00:22:46,820 --> 00:22:49,920
The locomotive fuel tank will hold anywhere from about 1,500

421
00:22:50,020 --> 00:22:53,460
gallons up to 4,500 gallons.

422
00:22:53,560 --> 00:22:56,560
At this facility, we will pump about 100,000 gallons

423
00:22:56,660 --> 00:22:59,580
of diesel fuel a day.

424
00:22:59,680 --> 00:23:03,480
As workers handle the basics from above,

425
00:23:03,580 --> 00:23:07,080
others are busy in the service pit below.

426
00:23:07,180 --> 00:23:08,780
As they're working up above, they're also

427
00:23:08,781 --> 00:23:12,780
We're also going to be changing brake shoes down below.

428
00:23:12,781 --> 00:23:16,780
Warren is going to be pulling this brake shoe out and going to be replacing it with the new brake shoe.

429
00:23:16,781 --> 00:23:20,780
He has to remove the brake key, take the old brake shoe out,

430
00:23:20,781 --> 00:23:26,780
replace it with the new brake shoe, and then drop the brake key back in that holds the brake shoe in place.

431
00:23:26,781 --> 00:23:28,212
And it's ready to go.

432
00:23:28,213 --> 00:23:32,780
Here we have a Warren brake shoe that is in need of being replaced.

433
00:23:32,781 --> 00:23:35,447
Here we have a new brake shoe that we will put in its place.

434
00:23:35,448 --> 00:23:36,780
The new brake shoe should last

435
00:23:36,781 --> 00:23:39,320
three to four weeks, depending on the amount of traffic

436
00:23:39,420 --> 00:23:42,780
the locomotive is involved in.

437
00:23:42,880 --> 00:23:45,220
Of course, there are times when some heavy lifting

438
00:23:45,320 --> 00:23:47,220
is required.

439
00:23:47,320 --> 00:23:50,520
For those cases, locomotives make a trip to the back shop,

440
00:23:50,620 --> 00:23:53,540
where mechanics carry out everything from cosmetic repair

441
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:56,540
to complete engine rebuilds.

442
00:23:56,640 --> 00:23:58,740
We do lots of accident damage.

443
00:23:58,840 --> 00:24:02,240
That could be crossing accidents that happen out

444
00:24:02,340 --> 00:24:03,840
on the line of road.

445
00:24:03,841 --> 00:24:08,840
We change main alternators, and we just do lots and lots of work,

446
00:24:08,940 --> 00:24:13,680
more or less rebuild the locomotive in that area.

447
00:24:13,780 --> 00:24:16,780
Once most locomotives are cleared to return to action,

448
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:19,180
they get a final sprucing up with a quick shower

449
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:23,300
in the CSX wash.

450
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:25,800
It gets a lot of the road film off of them,

451
00:24:25,900 --> 00:24:29,500
because after all, CSX is what's on the side of the locomotives.

452
00:24:29,600 --> 00:24:31,600
We're proud of our company, and we certainly

453
00:24:31,700 --> 00:24:33,840
want to look good in the public's eye.

454
00:24:33,841 --> 00:24:35,840
But sometimes looks can be deceiving.

455
00:24:36,380 --> 00:24:41,000
Ever see a 70 million ton ship steadied by one little engine that could?

456
00:24:42,680 --> 00:24:49,200
In 1896, 40,000 spectators watched two unmanned steam locomotives crash head-on

457
00:24:49,201 --> 00:24:51,980
during a publicity stunt in central Texas.

458
00:24:52,840 --> 00:24:56,920
Flying debris from the impact killed three spectators and injured six more.

459
00:24:57,540 --> 00:24:59,780
It was the last publicity stunt of its kind.

460
00:25:03,840 --> 00:25:07,340
Unlike the little engine that thought it could,

461
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:13,140
these mini locomotives along the Panama Canal know they can.

462
00:25:13,240 --> 00:25:17,680
Nearly 14,000 times a year, they slowly and steadily

463
00:25:17,780 --> 00:25:20,600
guide ships through the three locks that connect the Pacific

464
00:25:20,700 --> 00:25:22,600
and Atlantic Oceans.

465
00:25:26,100 --> 00:25:29,900
Here, size matters less than precision.

466
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,800
The locomotives measure just 13 feet high,

467
00:25:32,801 --> 00:25:38,040
15 feet high, 10 feet wide, and 35 feet long.

468
00:25:38,041 --> 00:25:40,340
But there's nothing small about the economic stakes

469
00:25:40,341 --> 00:25:41,640
of their mission.

470
00:25:41,641 --> 00:25:43,140
The way that we charge the ships

471
00:25:43,141 --> 00:25:44,840
depends on the size of the ship.

472
00:25:44,841 --> 00:25:47,540
There is a cruise ship on the right side.

473
00:25:47,541 --> 00:25:50,160
Those are the ones that pay the most on tolls,

474
00:25:50,161 --> 00:25:53,860
approximately $200,000 each.

475
00:25:53,861 --> 00:25:56,000
At these prices, the 40 shipping companies

476
00:25:56,001 --> 00:25:58,560
that send vessels through the canal every day

477
00:25:58,561 --> 00:26:01,600
expect to do it safely and efficiently.

478
00:26:01,601 --> 00:26:05,220
this 70,000-ton Panamanian cargo ship coming in from the Pacific.

479
00:26:05,880 --> 00:26:08,100
The chamber is very small for this ship,

480
00:26:08,440 --> 00:26:11,900
and it's a slow operation, and the locomotives are very critical

481
00:26:11,901 --> 00:26:13,900
for handling a ship of this size.

482
00:26:14,980 --> 00:26:17,140
As the ship approaches the lock's mouth,

483
00:26:17,800 --> 00:26:20,000
eight locomotives, four on the starboard side,

484
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:22,260
four to the port, move into position.

485
00:26:23,720 --> 00:26:25,880
The ship is 106 feet wide.

486
00:26:26,500 --> 00:26:28,460
The lock chamber is just 110.

487
00:26:28,461 --> 00:26:33,060
And for the next half mile, the little locos will be guiding

488
00:26:33,061 --> 00:26:35,860
the ship to the end of the lock.

489
00:26:35,861 --> 00:26:38,900
The carefully choreographed journey begins as canal ship

490
00:26:38,901 --> 00:26:42,500
personnel throw a line to be fastened to two steel cables

491
00:26:42,501 --> 00:26:46,820
that are attached to the winches on the side of the locomotives.

492
00:26:46,821 --> 00:26:50,220
The idea is to get the cables of the locomotives onto the

493
00:26:50,221 --> 00:26:54,820
ship. As you can see, there are two locomotives taking care of

494
00:26:54,821 --> 00:26:58,460
the bow of the ship. Then we're going to see the

495
00:26:58,461 --> 00:27:01,780
Two more taking care of the back of the ship, the stern.

496
00:27:02,400 --> 00:27:04,620
All eight locomotives are under the direction

497
00:27:04,621 --> 00:27:06,960
of a specially trained pilot aboard the ship

498
00:27:06,961 --> 00:27:08,540
who works for the canal.

499
00:27:09,900 --> 00:27:12,060
His first command to the locomotive operators

500
00:27:12,061 --> 00:27:14,100
is to pull their cables tight

501
00:27:14,101 --> 00:27:15,760
until the ship is securely tethered

502
00:27:15,761 --> 00:27:16,680
in the center of the lock.

503
00:27:18,460 --> 00:27:20,580
Then it's locomotives and ships ahoy

504
00:27:20,581 --> 00:27:23,800
as the pilot gradually powers his ship forward

505
00:27:23,801 --> 00:27:26,300
while directing the locomotives

506
00:27:26,301 --> 00:27:27,700
to move in unison with him.

507
00:27:28,460 --> 00:27:31,260
From here on, it's all about keeping the sides of the ship's

508
00:27:31,360 --> 00:27:35,900
steel hull off the cement walls that line the lock.

509
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,900
As you can see, the ship is very close to the wall.

510
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:41,000
The same thing is happening on the other side.

511
00:27:41,100 --> 00:27:43,900
So they should be able to coordinate with the pilot

512
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:47,020
to keep the ship as much in the center as they can.

513
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:49,120
There's only two feet on each side.

514
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:52,620
Any little mistake, you can rub the ship against the wall

515
00:27:52,720 --> 00:27:54,920
and cause damage.

516
00:27:55,020 --> 00:27:58,460
Top speed for the locomotives is 10 miles per hour.

517
00:27:58,461 --> 00:28:03,060
But with a ship this size, and so little room for error,

518
00:28:03,061 --> 00:28:06,060
they crawl along at about 3 miles per hour.

519
00:28:06,061 --> 00:28:08,860
Even at this speed, mistakes are costly.

520
00:28:08,861 --> 00:28:11,760
So to minimize any confusion between ship captain

521
00:28:11,761 --> 00:28:15,260
and locomotive operator, there are no conversations,

522
00:28:15,261 --> 00:28:18,260
just orders and action.

523
00:28:18,261 --> 00:28:22,260
The operator listens to the orders from the pilot via radio,

524
00:28:22,261 --> 00:28:24,260
and every time he hears a command,

525
00:28:24,261 --> 00:28:26,260
he has to ring the bell to acknowledge

526
00:28:26,261 --> 00:28:28,260
that he received the order.

527
00:28:28,261 --> 00:28:31,260
He operated the locomotive basically two main functions.

528
00:28:31,261 --> 00:28:34,260
Traction with this hand is moving the traction control,

529
00:28:34,261 --> 00:28:37,260
and on the left he has the controls for the cables,

530
00:28:37,261 --> 00:28:41,260
or two cables, one control for each cable.

531
00:28:41,261 --> 00:28:43,260
He's holding the ship in the center together

532
00:28:43,261 --> 00:28:46,260
with his partner on the other side.

533
00:28:46,261 --> 00:28:48,260
Asking number one center to coil.

534
00:28:48,261 --> 00:28:49,260
They don't have to do anything here,

535
00:28:49,261 --> 00:28:51,260
just keep the cables tight.

536
00:28:51,261 --> 00:28:54,260
He's moving ahead now a little bit.

537
00:28:54,261 --> 00:28:56,420
While the ship remains under its own power,

538
00:28:56,900 --> 00:28:58,940
the locomotives are propelled with electricity

539
00:28:58,941 --> 00:29:01,720
via a shoe that's connected to the rails.

540
00:29:02,340 --> 00:29:05,000
The rails are energized with 480 volts.

541
00:29:05,900 --> 00:29:06,860
In the middle of the track,

542
00:29:07,180 --> 00:29:09,320
a steel gear pulls the locomotive along.

543
00:29:10,900 --> 00:29:12,820
The electricity comes from below,

544
00:29:13,480 --> 00:29:15,420
inside a specially designed power station

545
00:29:15,421 --> 00:29:17,120
that runs the length of the track.

546
00:29:17,840 --> 00:29:19,840
We are inside of the machinery tunnel,

547
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:21,860
right underneath of the locomotive tracks

548
00:29:21,861 --> 00:29:24,020
that runs all along the log walls.

549
00:29:24,260 --> 00:29:26,260
And here is where the locomotives

550
00:29:26,261 --> 00:29:29,260
will get their power from.

551
00:29:29,261 --> 00:29:32,260
This is a transformer that brings the voltage down

552
00:29:32,261 --> 00:29:37,260
from 2,400 volts down to 480, the power required by the locomotive.

553
00:29:37,261 --> 00:29:43,260
These are the main breakers for the power that is fed to the locomotive above us.

554
00:29:43,261 --> 00:29:47,260
Up above, the Panamanian ship is now inside the lock chamber.

555
00:29:47,261 --> 00:29:50,260
As the lock fills with water and the ship rises,

556
00:29:50,261 --> 00:29:54,100
It's steady as she goes for the locomotive operators.

557
00:29:54,101 --> 00:29:55,600
During this time, the locomotives

558
00:29:55,601 --> 00:29:57,760
just have to keep the ship in the position

559
00:29:57,761 --> 00:29:59,100
with the cables tight.

560
00:29:59,101 --> 00:30:01,600
That way, we avoid hitting the walls

561
00:30:01,601 --> 00:30:04,100
and we keep the ship safe.

562
00:30:04,101 --> 00:30:07,920
Keeping a 140 million pound ship centered in the lock

563
00:30:07,921 --> 00:30:09,720
requires some tension.

564
00:30:09,721 --> 00:30:13,220
Most of it is focused on the cables.

565
00:30:13,221 --> 00:30:14,920
So the cables are routinely tested

566
00:30:14,921 --> 00:30:16,760
to withstand twice the amount of stress

567
00:30:16,761 --> 00:30:20,260
they'll ever need out in the lock.

568
00:30:20,261 --> 00:30:24,260
For the locomotive engineers, stress comes from the one thing

569
00:30:24,360 --> 00:30:26,760
they all fear, a dip in the drink.

570
00:30:26,860 --> 00:30:29,000
The cables are very tight, so the locomotives

571
00:30:29,100 --> 00:30:30,400
are pulling very strong.

572
00:30:30,500 --> 00:30:32,840
To avoid the locomotive overturning into the water,

573
00:30:32,940 --> 00:30:34,400
we have the safety wheels.

574
00:30:34,500 --> 00:30:36,080
That's why we use the safety wheels.

575
00:30:36,180 --> 00:30:38,480
They are attached to this piece of track,

576
00:30:38,580 --> 00:30:41,240
so the locomotive is always secure to the track.

577
00:30:41,340 --> 00:30:43,320
No matter how much force they put on the cables,

578
00:30:43,420 --> 00:30:45,580
they will always stay on the track.

579
00:30:45,680 --> 00:30:47,760
Of the thousands of passengers in the vehicle

580
00:30:47,761 --> 00:30:51,660
Of the thousands of passages during the canal's nearly 100

581
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:54,300
year history, just one locomotive

582
00:30:54,400 --> 00:30:57,000
has been pulled sideways into the canal.

583
00:30:57,100 --> 00:30:58,500
And that was due to human error.

584
00:30:58,600 --> 00:31:01,140
A closer look reveals the virtually accident-proof

585
00:31:01,240 --> 00:31:02,500
safeguarding system.

586
00:31:02,600 --> 00:31:04,580
Here we are underneath the locomotive

587
00:31:04,680 --> 00:31:06,180
in the maintenance pit.

588
00:31:06,280 --> 00:31:07,940
Here we can find the safety wheels.

589
00:31:08,040 --> 00:31:10,580
These are the ones that prevent the locomotive from tilting

590
00:31:10,680 --> 00:31:13,180
and going into the water.

591
00:31:13,280 --> 00:31:16,460
The gates of the lock are open.

592
00:31:16,461 --> 00:31:20,260
Like countless ships before it, the cargo carrier is about to

593
00:31:20,360 --> 00:31:23,260
leave its tight squeeze here for the open waters of the Pacific

594
00:31:23,360 --> 00:31:24,360
Ocean.

595
00:31:24,460 --> 00:31:27,200
The ship is ready to get out of the locks.

596
00:31:27,300 --> 00:31:29,400
We're reaching the end of the sidewalk.

597
00:31:29,500 --> 00:31:31,800
Three, so I'm going to hit up, thank you, number three.

598
00:31:31,900 --> 00:31:32,700
Number three.

599
00:31:32,800 --> 00:31:36,120
He just asked number three to release the wires and cast off.

600
00:31:36,220 --> 00:31:38,520
So he's going to cast off the number three.

601
00:31:38,620 --> 00:31:39,820
The guy's in front of us.

602
00:31:39,920 --> 00:31:41,420
Cast off, thank you, for sight.

603
00:31:41,520 --> 00:31:43,820
He just asked for sight us to cast off.

604
00:31:43,821 --> 00:31:44,660
to cast off.

605
00:31:44,661 --> 00:31:48,000
So he's going to release the wires and cast off.

606
00:31:48,001 --> 00:31:51,220
He's giving the signal so the deckhands can release

607
00:31:51,221 --> 00:31:54,460
the cable from the bit on the ship.

608
00:31:54,461 --> 00:31:57,540
They're going to let go any time.

609
00:31:57,541 --> 00:31:58,241
And here they come.

610
00:31:58,242 --> 00:32:00,040
One center.

611
00:32:00,041 --> 00:32:03,340
OK, so we're off the ship.

612
00:32:03,341 --> 00:32:05,680
45 minutes after it entered the locks,

613
00:32:05,681 --> 00:32:08,480
another vessel has completed safe passage

614
00:32:08,481 --> 00:32:11,620
for the little canal locomotives.

615
00:32:11,621 --> 00:32:16,860
A long line of customers is waiting at the lock's entrance.

616
00:32:16,861 --> 00:32:19,360
If you want to ride one of these locomotives,

617
00:32:19,361 --> 00:32:22,000
you'll need to take an elevator to get to it,

618
00:32:22,001 --> 00:32:26,040
some 600 feet underground.

619
00:32:26,041 --> 00:32:29,200
Since the Panama Canal opened in 1914,

620
00:32:29,201 --> 00:32:32,200
locomotives have guided more than 900,000 ships

621
00:32:32,201 --> 00:32:33,680
through the locks.

622
00:32:33,681 --> 00:32:37,640
The one-day record was set on February 29, 1968,

623
00:32:37,641 --> 00:32:40,280
when 65 ships made the passage.

624
00:32:41,620 --> 00:32:46,620
600 feet underground inside the Bailey Mine

625
00:32:46,720 --> 00:32:50,620
in southwestern Pennsylvania, a fleet of some

626
00:32:50,720 --> 00:32:53,660
of the most unusual locomotives is busy ferrying miners

627
00:32:53,760 --> 00:32:56,960
and equipment through a labyrinth of tunnels that

628
00:32:57,060 --> 00:32:58,380
spans hundreds of miles.

629
00:33:00,980 --> 00:33:05,080
Miners have been pulling coal out of the mine since 1984,

630
00:33:05,180 --> 00:33:06,580
and some of the original locomotives

631
00:33:06,680 --> 00:33:08,980
are still in operation.

632
00:33:08,981 --> 00:33:12,040
The fleet is powered by three different sources,

633
00:33:12,660 --> 00:33:16,780
external electrical, battery, and diesel.

634
00:33:18,560 --> 00:33:20,560
The electric locomotives are attached

635
00:33:20,561 --> 00:33:24,120
to an overhead trolley line and to its obvious limitations.

636
00:33:25,640 --> 00:33:28,840
This is a very old battery trolley unit.

637
00:33:29,900 --> 00:33:31,980
It's powered, it gets all its power

638
00:33:31,981 --> 00:33:33,480
from the overhead trolley line.

639
00:33:34,640 --> 00:33:37,800
That technology's been used in the mines for decades.

640
00:33:37,801 --> 00:33:39,700
The problem with the older technology

641
00:33:39,800 --> 00:33:43,440
is he has to be underneath this to operate.

642
00:33:43,540 --> 00:33:46,800
When he goes away from a trolley line, he's dead.

643
00:33:46,900 --> 00:33:48,840
Another drawback to the outdated trolley line

644
00:33:48,940 --> 00:33:52,280
is the 300 volts hanging from above,

645
00:33:52,380 --> 00:33:54,720
just waiting to take a bite out of anyone who accidentally

646
00:33:54,820 --> 00:33:56,380
touches it.

647
00:33:56,480 --> 00:33:59,420
Power outages create yet another problem.

648
00:33:59,520 --> 00:34:02,760
If you have any power problems or anything drops out,

649
00:34:02,860 --> 00:34:06,020
then that piece of equipment has no use.

650
00:34:06,021 --> 00:34:07,520
I mean, any power problem in the mine,

651
00:34:07,521 --> 00:34:09,860
or if you had to get out, you would have none.

652
00:34:09,861 --> 00:34:11,720
The mine's battery-powered locomotives

653
00:34:11,721 --> 00:34:15,060
eliminate the need for an overhead trolley line.

654
00:34:15,061 --> 00:34:19,460
But locomotive batteries don't keep going and going.

655
00:34:19,461 --> 00:34:21,660
You have to actually take it into a charging station

656
00:34:21,661 --> 00:34:22,680
and charge it.

657
00:34:22,681 --> 00:34:25,580
You're losing the use of your piece of equipment

658
00:34:25,581 --> 00:34:29,680
while you're actually charging it.

659
00:34:29,681 --> 00:34:32,220
Everything considered, the power source that most miners

660
00:34:32,221 --> 00:34:35,280
prefer may surprise you, diesel.

661
00:34:35,281 --> 00:34:36,980
Diesel is much safer.

662
00:34:36,981 --> 00:34:39,680
You no longer have the hazards of any short circuits

663
00:34:39,681 --> 00:34:42,620
in your coal mine and the potential for mine fires.

664
00:34:42,621 --> 00:34:45,220
With diesel, you're totally in control.

665
00:34:45,221 --> 00:34:48,820
I expect to have Bailey mine converted entirely, 100%

666
00:34:48,821 --> 00:34:51,120
to diesel, because I do believe diesel is the future

667
00:34:51,121 --> 00:34:54,340
for track-mounted equipment in the coal mine.

668
00:34:54,341 --> 00:34:57,440
Wondering how Bailey's miners co-exist deep underground

669
00:34:57,441 --> 00:34:59,440
with a herd of diesel-burning locomotives

670
00:34:59,441 --> 00:35:01,440
spewing out exhaust?

671
00:35:01,441 --> 00:35:04,840
Brookville Equipment Corporation in Brookville, Pennsylvania

672
00:35:04,841 --> 00:35:08,040
has been building underground diesel locomotives since 1980,

673
00:35:08,840 --> 00:35:11,500
and they have the answer to the diesel question.

674
00:35:12,880 --> 00:35:15,160
A lot needs to go into the safety and the health

675
00:35:15,161 --> 00:35:16,700
of the people working in that environment.

676
00:35:17,220 --> 00:35:20,140
This is a 170-horsepower diesel engine.

677
00:35:20,600 --> 00:35:22,940
It's MSHA-approved for underground use.

678
00:35:23,580 --> 00:35:26,680
The engine manufacturer has to take it through a series of tests

679
00:35:26,681 --> 00:35:29,480
where they measure the particulates,

680
00:35:29,481 --> 00:35:31,300
and they give it a certification.

681
00:35:32,300 --> 00:35:33,480
Brookville has also installed

682
00:35:33,481 --> 00:35:36,980
a state-of-the-art filtering system, sure to keep miners

683
00:35:36,981 --> 00:35:38,680
breathing easy.

684
00:35:38,681 --> 00:35:41,320
To run the diesel engines underground in a coal mine,

685
00:35:41,321 --> 00:35:44,020
you have to have an additional exhaust treatment system.

686
00:35:44,021 --> 00:35:47,020
The exhaust comes out and goes through a catalyst, which breaks

687
00:35:47,021 --> 00:35:49,020
apart the diesel particulate matter.

688
00:35:49,021 --> 00:35:53,340
The exhaust gas comes up into this filter housing, where a

689
00:35:53,341 --> 00:35:57,240
replaceable paper filter element is used to capture

690
00:35:57,241 --> 00:36:01,840
approximately 95% of the diesel soot that is emitted from the

691
00:36:01,841 --> 00:36:05,440
And that's why we don't smell diesel engine in here, diesel fuel.

692
00:36:05,540 --> 00:36:08,140
You put it in the box that the new one comes in,

693
00:36:08,240 --> 00:36:12,380
and it's acceptable to any landfill.

694
00:36:12,480 --> 00:36:15,180
For a typical Brookville diesel locomotive,

695
00:36:15,280 --> 00:36:21,500
nearly 2,000 parts are cut, drilled, forged, and assembled,

696
00:36:21,600 --> 00:36:25,800
while the diminutive frames get built separately.

697
00:36:25,900 --> 00:36:29,300
Engine sits in the front here, transmission in the center.

698
00:36:29,301 --> 00:36:31,300
You can see the two wheel wells,

699
00:36:31,301 --> 00:36:35,300
and these are grids for airflow

700
00:36:35,301 --> 00:36:37,300
and the operator compartment up front.

701
00:36:37,301 --> 00:36:41,300
These are 23-ton locomotives.

702
00:36:41,301 --> 00:36:43,300
Down in the Bailey Mine,

703
00:36:43,301 --> 00:36:46,300
a Brookville diesel-powered personal carrier

704
00:36:46,301 --> 00:36:49,300
is filling up for the morning commute.

705
00:36:49,301 --> 00:36:50,300
You can see the men in here,

706
00:36:50,301 --> 00:36:52,300
they're getting ready for their shift to begin.

707
00:36:52,301 --> 00:36:55,300
You can see the operator's station in this vehicle.

708
00:36:55,301 --> 00:36:57,300
He has all the controls that tell him

709
00:36:57,301 --> 00:37:00,060
The pilot controls that tell them full function is the engine,

710
00:37:00,061 --> 00:37:01,840
the brake and throttle.

711
00:37:01,841 --> 00:37:04,440
These guys got to go to work.

712
00:37:04,441 --> 00:37:08,140
They can go 10 to 12 miles an hour on good track.

713
00:37:08,141 --> 00:37:12,340
It's a whole lot faster than walking.

714
00:37:12,341 --> 00:37:14,760
Every day, 80 different underground vehicles

715
00:37:14,761 --> 00:37:16,760
travel throughout the mine.

716
00:37:16,761 --> 00:37:21,060
And every day, at least one of them derails.

717
00:37:21,061 --> 00:37:23,660
Brookville's got that covered, too.

718
00:37:23,661 --> 00:37:26,560
Now operators can get things back on track

719
00:37:26,561 --> 00:37:29,520
the rail walker.

720
00:37:29,521 --> 00:37:34,900
The old way is using a hand jack or a railroad jack.

721
00:37:34,901 --> 00:37:38,920
Very labor intensive and relatively dangerous.

722
00:37:38,921 --> 00:37:41,920
The new way is hydraulically.

723
00:37:41,921 --> 00:37:44,720
So what we did, we hydraulically lifted the locomotive

724
00:37:44,721 --> 00:37:46,560
by putting a foot down on the ground,

725
00:37:46,561 --> 00:37:49,420
raising the locomotive, and moving it sideways,

726
00:37:49,421 --> 00:37:51,360
positioning it back over the rail.

727
00:37:51,361 --> 00:37:53,220
It's moving back into position.

728
00:37:53,221 --> 00:37:58,300
the wheels being lowered gently in a controlled manner back down

729
00:37:58,301 --> 00:37:59,700
on the rail.

730
00:37:59,701 --> 00:38:02,000
With innovations like the rail walker

731
00:38:02,001 --> 00:38:04,460
and the clean-burning diesel engine,

732
00:38:04,461 --> 00:38:08,700
miners can move around safely and efficiently some 600 feet

733
00:38:08,701 --> 00:38:11,440
underground.

734
00:38:11,441 --> 00:38:13,800
Up above, there's a new locomotive that's

735
00:38:13,801 --> 00:38:16,280
designed to stay off the rails.

736
00:38:16,281 --> 00:38:19,920
And one American company is trying to catch up and pass

737
00:38:19,921 --> 00:38:23,220
the international competition.

738
00:38:23,221 --> 00:38:25,760
It takes the underground locomotives in the Bailey

739
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:29,460
Mine approximately one hour to haul supplies and workers

740
00:38:29,461 --> 00:38:32,260
for 10 miles between the entrance of the mine

741
00:38:32,261 --> 00:38:34,260
and the working section of the mine.

742
00:38:38,260 --> 00:38:42,260
Here's a game changer, the new world's fastest locomotive.

743
00:38:42,261 --> 00:38:45,380
It's called the Maglev, a high-speed train system

744
00:38:45,381 --> 00:38:47,760
being used commercially in China and Japan.

745
00:38:47,761 --> 00:38:48,461
in Japan.

746
00:38:51,460 --> 00:38:54,980
During a 2004 test run in Japan, the maglev rocketed

747
00:38:54,981 --> 00:39:00,440
to 361 miles per hour, the fastest speed ever recorded

748
00:39:00,441 --> 00:39:03,460
for a train.

749
00:39:03,461 --> 00:39:07,180
We know we said the French TGV was the fastest.

750
00:39:07,181 --> 00:39:09,060
But which one deserves the title depends

751
00:39:09,061 --> 00:39:11,320
on your definition of a locomotive.

752
00:39:11,321 --> 00:39:14,600
You see, an engine doesn't power the maglev.

753
00:39:14,601 --> 00:39:17,200
It's powered by the traffic.

754
00:39:17,201 --> 00:39:20,240
Paradoxically, the maglev train never even comes in contact

755
00:39:20,241 --> 00:39:20,980
with the rails.

756
00:39:22,840 --> 00:39:25,540
It's basically riding on a cushion of air.

757
00:39:25,920 --> 00:39:29,380
It's propelled by invisible magnetic forces.

758
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:33,640
The locomotive, if you will, is magnetically levitated,

759
00:39:33,900 --> 00:39:34,940
propelled, and guided.

760
00:39:35,320 --> 00:39:38,380
On General Atomic's test track in San Diego, California,

761
00:39:40,340 --> 00:39:43,000
engineers are working on a maglev system for America.

762
00:39:43,800 --> 00:39:46,520
If everything goes right, maglevs can be running

763
00:39:46,521 --> 00:39:50,420
in American cities within a decade.

764
00:39:50,421 --> 00:39:53,420
This prototype system is designed around the same basic law of

765
00:39:53,421 --> 00:39:56,220
physics as the maglev systems in China and Japan.

766
00:39:59,260 --> 00:40:03,480
Take some magnets, make a special track, apply power,

767
00:40:03,481 --> 00:40:06,080
and it's up, up, and away.

768
00:40:06,081 --> 00:40:09,280
The maglev track, or guideway, is lined with a cluster of

769
00:40:09,281 --> 00:40:13,280
electric power cables called a linear synchronous motor, or

770
00:40:13,281 --> 00:40:14,920
LSM.

771
00:40:14,921 --> 00:40:17,080
The train carriage is joined to a set of magnets

772
00:40:17,180 --> 00:40:19,620
that wrap around the track.

773
00:40:19,720 --> 00:40:22,100
When the linear synchronous motor is energized,

774
00:40:22,200 --> 00:40:24,700
it generates a moving magnetic field,

775
00:40:24,800 --> 00:40:29,260
creating a magnetic wave that pulls the train carriage forward.

776
00:40:29,360 --> 00:40:34,180
To increase speed, more power is added to the LSM.

777
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:36,480
As the carriage moves forward, the electricity

778
00:40:36,580 --> 00:40:38,280
shuts down behind it.

779
00:40:38,380 --> 00:40:40,820
That leaves the issue of levitation.

780
00:40:40,920 --> 00:40:43,740
Let me tell you about how the levitation system works.

781
00:40:43,741 --> 00:40:47,440
We get levitation by virtue of currents that are induced

782
00:40:47,540 --> 00:40:48,640
in this track.

783
00:40:48,740 --> 00:40:52,280
The magnets are in this wraparound structure.

784
00:40:52,380 --> 00:40:54,480
They're inside these cans.

785
00:40:54,580 --> 00:40:58,880
And we use a set of magnets for levitation.

786
00:40:58,980 --> 00:41:01,300
For the levitation to occur, the magnets

787
00:41:01,400 --> 00:41:06,200
are aligned in a formation called the Hallback Array.

788
00:41:06,300 --> 00:41:09,540
The way the levitation system here is going to work

789
00:41:09,541 --> 00:41:14,540
is that these arrows indicate the polarity of the magnets.

790
00:41:14,541 --> 00:41:18,040
That basic configuration is nothing more

791
00:41:18,041 --> 00:41:22,040
than each of these magnets is rotated by 45 degrees.

792
00:41:22,041 --> 00:41:25,840
What it winds up doing is it produces a sine wave

793
00:41:25,841 --> 00:41:27,840
that is focused on the track.

794
00:41:27,841 --> 00:41:30,940
It goes in through here, it returns off here,

795
00:41:30,941 --> 00:41:33,640
it hugs the backside and comes out,

796
00:41:33,641 --> 00:41:37,640
and it tends to focus the magnetic field on the track

797
00:41:37,641 --> 00:41:39,140
where you want it.

798
00:41:39,141 --> 00:41:40,900
In layman's terms, what that means

799
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:44,140
is that the wheels are going to lift off the track.

800
00:41:44,240 --> 00:41:47,980
All the magnets need is some forward motion.

801
00:41:48,080 --> 00:41:51,980
On the track, the electric cables provide it.

802
00:41:52,080 --> 00:41:55,700
In here, the spinning wheel induces the power.

803
00:41:55,800 --> 00:41:58,000
And actually, what you're seeing right now

804
00:41:58,100 --> 00:42:00,600
is that basic principle of levitation.

805
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:03,240
We're levitated to well over an inch,

806
00:42:03,340 --> 00:42:06,000
and the levitation force is maintained

807
00:42:06,100 --> 00:42:08,640
as long as there's forward motion.

808
00:42:08,641 --> 00:42:11,840
When we're coming into a station and we're slowing down,

809
00:42:12,140 --> 00:42:17,460
then we're going to gradually get less and less of an air gap

810
00:42:17,461 --> 00:42:20,020
until we're almost at the station here.

811
00:42:20,021 --> 00:42:23,680
As we come in, we're just about now landing on the wheels

812
00:42:23,681 --> 00:42:25,260
and we come to a stop.

813
00:42:26,120 --> 00:42:28,840
Build up enough speed, and it's time for the landing wheels

814
00:42:28,841 --> 00:42:29,580
to lift off.

815
00:42:30,820 --> 00:42:34,880
When the speed of the vehicle reaches approximately 10 miles

816
00:42:34,881 --> 00:42:38,040
per hour, enough current is induced in the track

817
00:42:38,041 --> 00:42:41,940
that it produces levitation of the entire structure,

818
00:42:42,040 --> 00:42:47,140
including the magnets as well as the wheels here.

819
00:42:47,240 --> 00:42:49,780
By levitating the carriage above the track,

820
00:42:49,880 --> 00:42:51,920
you've got a frictionless system that's

821
00:42:52,020 --> 00:42:56,300
the key to generating speeds up to 350 miles per hour.

822
00:42:56,400 --> 00:42:58,520
Like the maglevs in China and Japan,

823
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:03,300
the proposed system would require its own dedicated track.

824
00:43:03,400 --> 00:43:07,340
But the separate track is also one of maglevs advantages.

825
00:43:07,341 --> 00:43:13,180
no rail crossings. There's also no need for an engineer.

826
00:43:13,280 --> 00:43:16,140
The system is completely automated, run from a control

827
00:43:16,240 --> 00:43:19,280
room where all operators have to do is program the motor to

828
00:43:19,380 --> 00:43:23,080
induce the right amount of power ahead of the carriage.

829
00:43:23,180 --> 00:43:25,900
But the General Atomic's proposed system has one major

830
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:28,900
difference from the high-speed Asian versions.

831
00:43:29,000 --> 00:43:32,040
We're focusing our technology on lower speed

832
00:43:32,140 --> 00:43:35,700
operation, urban systems, up to about 100 miles per hour.

833
00:43:35,701 --> 00:43:39,740
We feel that there is a real market for alleviating much

834
00:43:39,840 --> 00:43:44,480
of the congestion that we find in many of our cities.

835
00:43:44,580 --> 00:43:47,920
By late 2009, General Atomics hopes

836
00:43:48,020 --> 00:43:51,140
to begin construction of a maglev demonstration system

837
00:43:51,240 --> 00:43:55,780
on the campus of the California University of Pennsylvania.

838
00:43:55,880 --> 00:43:59,460
Building the maglev system will cost up to $40 million

839
00:43:59,560 --> 00:44:01,160
per mile.

840
00:44:01,260 --> 00:44:03,220
But if it does get built, America

841
00:44:03,221 --> 00:44:06,320
will finally be ready to join the international community

842
00:44:06,321 --> 00:44:09,820
of high-performance passenger locomotives.

